<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Dabuya</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">7th century</span> · <a href="/centuries/7th-century">7th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Dabuya was a ruler in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tabaristan">Tabaristan</a>, northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>, in the 7th century, a son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gil-gavbara">Gil Gavbara</a> and the figure from whom the Dabuyid dynasty took its name. According to the historian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibn-isfandiyar">Ibn Isfandiyar</a> his descendants ruled the lowlands of Tabaristan as ispahbads until the Abbasid conquest of 759–760, while his brother Paduspan founded the Paduspanid line in the mountains.</p></div>
